[{:db/id -1
 :adr/id "adr-2609071100-capacity-that-folds-prefill-into-throughput"
 :adr/title "A capacity number that divides completion tokens by wall clock is measuring the prompt, not the fleet"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-07"
 :adr/body "## Context\n\n`https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1/models` published, for `murakumo-main`\n(Qwen3.8-27B Q4_K_M), `capacity.text[output].value = 60` tokens per minute,\n`max_concurrency = 2`, and `capacity-measured-aggregate-tok-s = 1.04` at\nconcurrency 1, measured 2026-09-05. The accompanying `capacity-method` recorded\na thirty-five-fold degradation from 35.56 tok/s at concurrency 4 six days\nearlier, named \"b70 head queueing server-side\" as the cause, and said it was\nflagged for the operator rather than papered over\n(`network-awai/cloud-murakumo-api` commit 61c1d8b).\n\nMeasured 2026-09-07 on the b70 head itself rather than through the public\nboundary, over every completed task in a 24-hour window:\n\n- decode 20.2-20.9 ms per token, i.e. 47.8-49.4 tokens/second\n- prompt eval 975-1,220 tokens/second\n- host load average 0.35, llama-server active, `/health` ok\n\nThe head is not degraded. One task in that window spent 13,275 ms evaluating a\n12,951-token prompt before its first byte, with `n_prompt_tokens_cache: 0`.\n\n## Finding\n\nThe published figure is `sum(usage.completion_tokens) / wall_clock`, and the\nwall clock includes prefill. Sixteen output tokens behind a 13-second uncached\nprefill publish as about 1.2 tok/s while the GPU decodes at 48. The number is\ntherefore dominated by prompt length and prompt-cache state, not by serving\ncapacity, and **two such numbers are not comparable to each other** unless the\nprompt was the same. 08-31's 35.56 and 09-05's 1.04 were not.\n\nThe concurrency-2 timeouts have a separate and ordinary cause: the unit\n`murakumo-b70-llama.service` runs `--parallel 1`, one slot, so a second\nconcurrent request waits for the first, and a first request taking ~25 s pushes\nthe second past the edge timeout. The published `max_concurrency` of 2 is twice\nthe real slot count.\n\nSo one published value folds three independent facts together -- decode rate,\nprefill cost, and queueing behind a single slot -- and a prompt-cache miss\nreads as a fleet collapse. This is ADR-2608136000's shape at the level of a\npublic capacity declaration: a measurement that cannot distinguish two\nsituations returns the same value for both.\n\nNotably the honest instinct made it worse. The 09-05 commit deliberately\npublished the unflattering number rather than hiding it, and that is right; but\nan unexamined bad number published humbly is still an unexamined bad number,\nand it understated the owner's own fleet by roughly 45x for two days.\n\n## Decision\n\nA capacity declaration must publish decode rate and prefill rate as distinct\nvalues, with a concurrency figure that matches the real slot count, and must\nsay where each was taken -- server-side `llama-server` timings and end-to-end\nmeasurements through the public boundary answer different questions. Both are\nlegitimate; publishing one under the other's name is not. Any aggregate that\nmixes phases must be labelled prompt-length-dependent so nobody compares two of\nthem.\n\n## Status of the work\n\nRecorded, not yet fixed. The re-cut declaration, the gad and xavier\nmeasurements needed to pick pool-wide numbers, and the diagnosis of the zero\nprompt-cache hits are the standing backlog of the Hermes profile\n`murakumo-infer-maturity` (cron job 9ea532317c62, every 4h, workdir this\nsuperproject). The public blog post at\n`https://murakumo.cloud/blog/2026-murakumo-vs-vllm-sglang-llamacpp` already\nstates the correction, so the published page and the published API disagree\nuntil item 1 of that backlog lands -- deliberately, because the page's whole\nargument is that the operator publishes its own unflattering measurements.\n\n## Reproduction\n\n```\nssh jun@100.119.10.43 'journalctl -u murakumo-b70-llama.service --no-pager \\\n  --since \"-24 hours\" | grep -E \"eval time =\"'\ncurl -s https://api.murakumo.cloud/v1/models\n```\n\nDo not copy today's numbers forward. Run the commands.\n\n## 改訂 2026-09-07 — この ADR の主因の帰属が誤っていた\n\n上の分析は b70 を測って書かれている。b70 の 47.8-49.4 tok/s は正しいが、\n**`murakumo-main` の主経路は b70 ではない。** endpoint は gad:8090 で、\n2026-09-07 時点でそのポートは `murakumo-ring.service` ——\n9 台の Mac mini と head からなる、稼働中の 10 参加者 llama.cpp RPC ring\n(`--split-mode layer --tensor-split 3,3,3,3,4,3,3,3,3,12 -ngl 999 -c 524288\n--parallel 2`) である。\n\n7 日間 2,021 タスクの実測: ring の decode 中央値 **10.38 tok/s**\n(p25 6.56 / p75 15.31 / min 0.13)、prefill 中央値 170 tok/s。\n09-07 の 1 タスクは 14,489 トークンの prefill に 83,707 ms、その後 29 トークンの\n生成に 35,460 ms —— end-to-end 0.24 tok/s。\n\nしたがって 1.04 の寄与は 1 つではなく 2 つで、**大きい方は会計上の選択ではない**:\n\n1. `sum(completion_tokens)/wall_clock` に prefill が入っている（この ADR が\n   述べた欠陥。実在し、まだ直っていない）\n2. 既定経路そのものが、同じ pool 内の単ノードより **1 トークンあたり約 4.6 倍\n   遅い** —— 1 GbE の上に分散しているため\n\nADR-2607211600 系が記録した 2026-07-05 の「ring 12.7 tok/s 対 standalone\n61.5 tok/s」は履歴ではなく**現在の本番既定**であり、2,021 タスクで\n10.4 対 48 として再測された。ring は単ノードが保持できない 524k の context を\n買っており、その対価はトークン毎秒で支払われている。今週まで誰もその値段を\n測っていなかった。\n\n⚠ **なぜ間違えたかを残す。** pool の 1 メンバーだけを測って、その結果を pool\n全体の説明として書いた。`/ready` は `pool-available {gad, b70, xavier}` を\n返しており、どのメンバーが答えたかは最初から読めた。「境界ではなく機械そのものを\n測れ」という自分の結論を、**測る機械を 1 台間違える**という形で破っている。\n公開ページ (`https://murakumo.cloud/blog/2026-murakumo-vs-vllm-sglang-llamacpp`)\nにも同じ訂正を、消さずに追記した。"}]
